  "summary_md": "Amid consumer electronics and education, AI is the dominant fault line. BOXL’s double-digit growth and raised guidance highlight enterprise demand for AI content management, while SKIL’s AI-native platform is winning early adopters even as divestitures mask segment profitability. Conversely, CHGG’s academic business is crumbling under free AI tools, making its B2B AI training pivot an uncertain turnaround. Strategic repositioning is broad: GPRO is cutting staff to pivot from cameras to defense and pro filmmaking; UEIC has installed an interim CEO and faces activist demands to surface asset value; and UDMY’s Coursera merger commits to a subscription-first AI upskilling model following pre-deal losses. A parallel thread is capital return, with TBCH refinancing specifically to expand buybacks, while speculative trading continues to drive KOSS and KIDZ far from underlying fundamentals.",
